# Line endings are a property of the checkout, not of the content. This repo had
# no .gitattributes, so Git stored whatever the writing machine produced: five
# text files sit at HEAD with CRLF (`web/src/api/client.ts` at 3042 lines) while
# their neighbours in the same directory are LF. Nothing in the tree declared
# which was intended, so the only way to tell was to open the blob.
#
# The cost is not cosmetic. With `core.autocrlf=false`, touching a CRLF file
# rewrites every line of it: `jailbreak_sync_test.go`, normalised to LF by hand
# in #1362, showed as 659 insertions and 659 deletions to say nothing.
#
# This file and the renormalised blobs below are one change, and they have to be:
# `eol=lf` makes Git check every text file out as LF, so a blob still stored as
# CRLF reads as modified on every Linux checkout until its blob is converted.
# Splitting them would leave the tree dirty between the two.

# LF in the repository, LF on every checkout. `text=auto` still lets Git decide
# what counts as text rather than naming every extension, so a type omitted
# below is handled rather than stored as an opaque blob.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Named explicitly where a wrong guess is expensive: a misextension here either
# corrupts a binary or bloats a text file.
*.go text eol=lf
*.ts text eol=lf
*.svelte text eol=lf
*.js text eol=lf
*.mjs text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.css text eol=lf
*.html text eol=lf
*.svg text eol=lf
*.ps1 text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
*.txt text eol=lf
*.mod text eol=lf
*.sum text eol=lf
*.example text eol=lf
*.webmanifest text eol=lf

# Explicit binary. Without these, `text=auto` sniffs bytes and an unlucky
# sequence in a font or an icon turns it into a permanently damaged text diff.
# `default.pgo` is the reason `.pgo` is named: it is a gzip-compressed PGO
# profile, not a Go source file, so a text rule on the extension corrupts it.
*.pgo binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary

# CHANGELOG.md is the one file deliberately left alone: it ships CRLF, and
# `make changelog-merge` rewrites the whole file at every release anyway, so a
# conversion here buys nothing and §7.6 forbids this path appearing in a PR
# diff. `-text` keeps it byte-identical in the blob and on every checkout, which
# is what stops it showing as modified on Linux while its neighbours convert.
CHANGELOG.md -text